Z||000000||rodmcneill||05-22-2001||12:34 AM||||Has anyone tried the Popular Mechanics CD rom auto repair thingy? My local K Mart has them on sale for $14.00. I guess it allows you to access online repair manuals. One car type per CD. The GM one goes up to 87 and the next one starts at 88 up. I have an 86 and a 88 GT will I need to buy both CD's? What kind of feedback do you have this product? Should I just buy the factory repair manuals for the specific years?||||reg||5
Z||000001||MrPBody||05-22-2001||08:55 AM||||I have a CD for '88 Jeep Cherokee.  Based on that, I think the CD is good for access to service bulletins; however, for general repairs I prefer a service manual.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||ThatRickGuy||05-22-2001||10:13 AM||||We had the entire series at my old high school shop. I can't remember much from it, but I do remember it being useful for some little things when I was helping some friends w/ their cars.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-22-2001||10:49 AM||||The PM CD is an ALLdata product. That said...<P>If you have one CD that covers both cars... You can unlock more than one car. They will however charge for it.<P>In your case you'll need both disks...<P>I've used ALLdata, I Have Not used the PM retail product. Overall I found the ALLdata info most usefull for the 87+ DIS 4 cyl. This motor is not covered at all in the Haynes book. The older 4 Cyl and the V6 systems are covered well in the retail service books.<P>The only other choice for the DIS motor is the GM/Helms shop book.||||reg||1
